.lua
book.chapter(4, "Values, arithmetic, and evaluation")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Primitive values are already typed"
.pp
The primitive types are Unit, Bool, Int, Float, and String. Unit has the single
value (), useful when an operation needs an explicit argument but no input
information, or when its effect is the interesting result. Bool has true and
false. Int is signed 64-bit two's-complement arithmetic; Float is binary64.
String stores immutable bytes. Later chapters add tuples, lists, records,
constructors, functions, and opaque host values to this initial vocabulary.
.pp
Do not confuse unit with an empty list or an absent value. The empty list has
an element type that can be instantiated at use; None is an Option constructor
representing absence; unit is a successful value containing no further
information. These choices communicate different contracts. A printer returns
unit because it performed an effect, while an indexed lookup returns Option
because a requested element might not exist.
.sh 2 "Integers obey a bounded arithmetic model"
.pp
Ordinary integer addition, subtraction, multiplication, and negation wrap.
This is a language-level rule implemented consistently across the hosts, not
permission for a C++ embedding callback to perform undefined signed overflow.
When the mathematical result is required, checked library operations return
Option. An absent result then means that the signed 64-bit interval could not
represent the answer. Parsing a literal outside that interval is instead a
source diagnostic; it does not wrap into another literal value.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "integer division" (7 / 3) 2
Test.equal "truncates toward zero" ((~7) / 3) (~2)
Test.equal "signed remainder" ((~7) % 3) (~1)
Test.equal "wrap at boundary" (Int.max_value + 1) Int.min_value
Test.equal "checked boundary" (Int.checked_add Int.max_value 1) None
Test.equal "checked success" (Int.checked_mul 6 7) (Some 42)]=], "Arithmetic contracts at ordinary and boundary values")
book.code("metaml-error:M0402", [=[1 / 0]=], "Integer division by zero is a diagnostic")
.endlua
.pp
Division truncates toward zero, which differs from mathematical floor for a
negative quotient. The remainder follows the dividend's sign in the familiar
truncating convention. If an algorithm requires a nonnegative modular residue,
state and implement that requirement explicitly. The most negative integer
also has no positive counterpart in the same signed range, so applying abs
does not magically turn bounded integers into mathematical integers.
.sh 2 "Floating point is an approximation with explicit boundaries"
.pp
Binary64 can represent many useful values exactly, but most decimal fractions
are approximations. Conversion from a sufficiently large integer can lose
precision even though the integer itself fits in Int. Use Float.of_int to make
that boundary visible. Arithmetic constraints do not imply unrestricted
automatic coercion between every numeric type. A mixed representation should
be a deliberate design choice, especially at a host or file-format boundary.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[average a b := (Float.of_int a + Float.of_int b) / 2.0
Test.equal "explicit conversion" (average 3 4) 3.5
Test.check "finite" (Float.is_finite (average 3 4))
Test.check "NaN is unordered" (not (Float.nan == Float.nan))
Test.equal "truncate" (Float.truncate 3.9) 3]=], "Conversions and classification")
.endlua
.pp
NaN is unequal to itself. Infinity and negative zero are meaningful values as
well, and canonical rendering preserves relevant distinctions. A comparison
helper is not automatically a mathematically useful total ordering on values
containing NaN. For numerical tests, prefer a domain-appropriate tolerance
unless exact equality is part of the contract. For identifiers, money, and
array sizes, do not select floating point merely because decimal text is
convenient to display.
.sh 2 "Evaluation order determines effects"
.pp
Function arguments are eager: their expressions are evaluated before the
function body receives values. A conditional evaluates its condition and then
only the selected branch. Boolean conjunction and disjunction short-circuit.
These are different mechanisms. Passing an expensive fallback to an ordinary
helper evaluates that fallback even when the helper eventually ignores it;
putting it in the unselected branch of a conditional does not.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[safe_divide x y := if y == 0 then None else Some (x / y)
Test.equal "avoided division" (safe_divide 7 0) None
Test.equal "selected division" (safe_divide 7 2) (Some 3)
Test.equal "short conjunction" (false && (1 / 0 == 0)) false
Test.equal "short disjunction" (true || (1 / 0 == 0)) true]=], "Selection controls which expressions run")
book.algorithm("Evaluate an ordinary call", {
    "Evaluate the function expression to a callable value.",
    "Evaluate each argument expression in evaluator order.",
    "Bind the resulting values to the callable's parameters.",
    "If insufficient arguments are supplied, return a partial application.",
    "Otherwise evaluate the body in its captured environment."
})
.endlua
.pp
A closure captures an environment so its body can run later. That does not
mean it memoizes the result or delays all input expressions. This distinction
is central to the library chapters: Option.map delays its callback body until
there is a value to map, but evaluating the callback expression itself is still
ordinary eager evaluation. Similarly, constructing a list evaluates its
elements rather than creating a lazy sequence of promised computations.
.sh 2 "Equality, ordering, and display"
.pp
Compound values compare structurally where supported. Lists compare elements,
tuples compare components, records include their labels and values, and
constructors include their tags and arguments. Functions cannot be compared
meaningfully by the generic operations and can cause a runtime diagnostic.
Opaque host resources generally have identity semantics rather than a deep
comparison of external state. A type that contains such a value inherits those
limitations when a generic comparison reaches it.
.pp
Core.show returns canonical inspection text. A string appears quoted, a
function appears as a function marker, and a constructor displays its name.
That makes show excellent for debugging but unsuitable as a universal storage
format. A persistent protocol needs an explicit schema and a defined decoder.
The distinction becomes particularly important when a value contains an
abstract type, a mutable handle, or an integer that a downstream JSON consumer
would convert to an imprecise floating number.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
The Value representation, evaluator arithmetic branches, and to_string in
MetaML.hpp define the core behavior; metalib/int and metalib/float provide
named interfaces. Test the boundary between wrapping and checked arithmetic by
adding one to the maximum integer. Then write a division function returning a
Result with an informative error. Explain why an eager default argument cannot
replace its conditional when computing that default would itself divide by
zero. The explanation should refer to evaluation, not just to types.
